<title>USA: TOBACCO-FLORIDA  RBR PASSES.</title>
<headline>TOBACCO-FLORIDA  RBR PASSES.</headline>
<byline>Stephen Rothman</byline>
<dateline>WEST PALM BEACH, Fla. 1997-08-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Attorneys involved in Florida's $1 billion tobacco trial agreed Thursday that testimony could get under way by Labor Day on September 1.</p>
<p>&quot;That is possible. It is do-able,&quot; said Robert Montgomery, the Florida attorney charged with helping choose the six-member jury and nine alternates in the first state Medicaid case to come to trial.</p>
<p>&quot;The way the judge has things set up now that is a very reasonable date,&quot; Montgomery said during a break in the screening of Palm Beach County residents for jury service.  </p>
<p>Under the jury selection rules that both sides have agreed to, each side has 36 preemptory challenges they can use to eliminate potential jury candidates for the six member jury.</p>
<p>Each side also has nine preemtory challenges that they can exercise to eliminate each of the nine potential alternate jurors.</p>
</text>
